Troubleshooting network issues isn’t guesswork — it’s about knowing which tool to use at each layer.

Question: How do you use commands like ping, traceroute, telnet, nc (netcat), or curl to debug network issues?

Here’s my layered approach 👇

🔹 ping – Check basic connectivity (is the host reachable?).
🔹 traceroute – Find the path packets take & where they drop.
🔹 telnet – Test if a specific TCP port (22, 80, etc.) is open.
🔹 nc (netcat) – More flexible: test TCP/UDP ports, banner grabbing, even file transfer.
🔹 curl – Verify the application layer (HTTP/HTTPS, APIs, etc.)

Example:
When SSH wasn’t working on a server, I started with ping (server reachable ✅), then traceroute (path fine ✅). Using nc -zv server 22, I found port 22 was blocked — a firewall rule issue. Fixing that solved it instantly.
